## Compact and considered

Fifteen square metres, arranged so that nothing in them is wasted. Two single beds stand against a panel of soft geometric wallpaper in sage green, with the deep red patterned carpet running underneath and a reading light to hand.

The rest of the room is built around that wall. A slim black console runs along one side with the kettle and the coffee machine on it, cups and a small plant beside them, and there is room underneath for a case you would rather not have open on the floor. The flat-screen TV is wall-mounted and out of the way. Tall windows with full-length curtains look out into the plane trees on Ceintuurbaan, and the deep red patterned carpet is the one flash of colour in an otherwise quiet room.

The bathroom is white-tiled with a quarter-round corner shower, a wall-hung basin, a mirror with a light above it, and a heated rail that will have a towel dry by morning.

This is the smallest room in the house and it is priced accordingly, which makes it the sensible choice if you plan to be out most of the day. Step through the front door and the Albert Cuypmarkt is at the end of the street, the Museumplein is five minutes on the tram, and the canal ring starts a quarter of an hour north on foot.

Free WiFi throughout, and tea and coffee are always in the room. Reception is downstairs around the clock.
